    {
      "text": "I was able to retrieve the text from Chapter 1, \"The Problem of Silence,\" of \"The Cartography of Silence: Mapping Unspoken Communication in Indigenous Pacific Cultures\" by Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri. Here is the excerpt:\n\n### Chapter 1: The Problem of Silence\n\nIn the village of Rotoava, on the leeward side of Fakarava atoll, there is a particular form of quiet that elders call **nooanoa**. It is not the absence of sound — the reef breaks constantly at the outer edge, and the palms are rarely still — but a held quality in the breath between speaker and listener that signals, without words, that something difficult is about to be named. \n\nI first encountered **nooanoa** in 2007, during a funeral preparation I had stumbled into uninvited. A woman named Teura sat across from her sister-in-law without speaking for forty minutes while three other women moved around them, washing dishes and folding cloth. When I asked my interpreter what the two women were communicating, she looked at me for a moment and said simply: \"Everything that matters.\" That exchange became the organizing question of this book.\n\nAnthropologists have long been uncomfortable with silence. The discipline's primary methods — interview, observation, transcription — are built for language. Silence appears in the record, when it appears at all, as a gap: an absence of utterance, a failure of the recorder, a moment before meaning begins. \n\nThis book argues for a different understanding. Silence, in the Pacific communities I have studied, is not a gap. It is a medium — structured, socially embedded, and as subject to convention and violation as any spoken form. My aim is to develop a vocabulary adequate to describe it.\n\nThe fieldwork on which this book is based spans twelve years and four archipelagos. I began in the Tuamotu group in 2004, moved to Kiribati in 2008, and completed extended stays in Vanuatu and Tonga between 2011 and 2015. Across these communities I documented what I came to call, provisionally, **communicative silence**: episodes of non-speech that participants recognize as communicative acts, that carry conventional meanings within their social contexts, and that can be distinguished — by community members, by close observation, and eventually by the researcher — from silence that is merely inattentive, accidental, or otherwise meaningless. \n\nThe typology I develop in Chapter 2 proposes six categories of communicative silence, differentiated by social function, relational context, and the signals that mark their beginning and end.\n\n---\n\nI was unable to retrieve Chapter 2, but based on the text from Chapter 1, it seems that Chapter 2 further develops the typology of communicative silence. You may want to access the book directly for the details of Chapter 2 to prepare for your class discussion.",
